}\]

If $\scr{M}$ is a $(\scr{C},\scr{D})$-bimodule and $F\colon \scr{A}\to \scr{C}$ and $G\colon \scr{B}\to \scr{D}$ are enriched functors, 
${_F\scr{M}_G}$ is the $(\scr{A},\scr{B})$-bimodule defined as the composite 
\[
  \scr{A}^{\text{op}}\otimes \scr{B}\xrightarrow{F\otimes G}\scr{C}^{\text{op}}\otimes \scr{D}\xrightarrow{\scr{M}}\scr{V}
\]
If $\scr{Q}$ is an $(\scr{C},\scr{C})$-bimodule and $F$ is as above, we have the following composite 
\[
  \rbm{\epsilon}{F}{\scr{C}}{\scr{Q}}\colon {_F\scr{Q}_F}\odot {_F\scr{C}}
  \cong {_F\scr{C}}\odot \scr{Q}\odot \scr{C}_F\odot {_F\scr{C}} 
  \xrightarrow{\rsm{\epsilon}{_F\scr{C}}{\scr{C}_F}{\scr{Q}}}{_F\scr{C}}\odot \scr{Q} 
\]
and the corresponding maps for $\rbm{\eta}{F}{\scr{C}}{\scr{Q}}$, $\lbm{\epsilon}{F}{\scr{C}}{\scr{Q}}$, and $\lbm{\eta}{F}{\scr{C}}{\scr{Q}}$.    The are also versions of these maps for monoids.


